-- title:
--   ̄ j(q^{Nℓ}) = ̄ j(q^N)^ℓ in characteristic ℓ
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a commutative ring, let $N$ be a natural number that is nonzero, and let $\ell$ be a prime such that $K$ has characteristic $\ell$. Write $\bar j(q)$ for the Laurent series `jqModC K` over $K$, namely the product of the Hahn series $q^{-1}$ (the single term $1$ in degree $-1$) with the image in $K[[q]] \subseteq K((q))$ of the integral power series `jNum` under the ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z} \to K$; and for $M \neq 0$ write $\bar j(q^M) =$ `jqNModC K M` for its image under `qExpand K M`, the ring endomorphism of $K((q))$ obtained by pushing the support forward along multiplication by $M$ on $\mathbb{Z}$ (an order-preserving injection), that is, the substitution $q \mapsto q^{M}$. The assertion is the identity $$\bar j(q^{N\ell}) = \bar j(q^{N})^{\ell}$$ in $K((q))$, i.e. `jqNModC K (N * ℓ) = (jqNModC K N) ^ ℓ`. No hypothesis is imposed relating $\ell$ to $N$; in particular $\ell$ may divide $N$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion form of the statement that, modulo $\ell$, the $N\ell$-th expansion of $j$ is the $\ell$-th power of the $N$-th one — the series-level shadow of the fact that one branch of the reduction of $X_0(N\ell) \to X_0(N)$ is Frobenius. It is used in the analysis of the fields generated by such expansions and in the construction of prolongation tuples and integral models in the place-specialisation part of the development.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_jqNModC_mul_eq_pow.lean

